  "inscriptions": [
    {
      "inscription": "紫篔生李升識\n[印] 李氏子雲",
      "inscription_translation": "Artist's signature and seal: \nZi yun sheng, Li Sheng signed. \n[seal] Li shi zi yun.",
      "inscription_remark": null,
      "sortorder": 1
    },
    {
      "inscription": "4 colophons and 12 additional seals: \n\nThe \"Heart\" Sutra (Maha Prajnaparamita) text written by Wu Yuqing 吳餘慶 (active early 1400s); \n2 seals of Xu Qianxue 徐乾學 (1631–1694); \n3 colophons, one dated 1929 and two dated 1936, and 9 seals of Wu Hufan 吳湖帆 (1894–1968); \n1 colophon, dated 1932, of Chen Dingshan; 1 seal unidentified.",
      "inscription_translation": null,
      "inscription_remark": null,
      "sortorder": 2
    },
    {
      "inscription": null,
      "inscription_translation": "An authentic work by Li Sheng; I have seen no other besides this scroll. Although I do not dare to definitively establish it as a unique surviving example, it is indeed a renowned work of which few remain. The brushwork is rather close to that of the Song painting, Mountain Market in Stream and Mist, in the Tanaka collection in Japan, which may also be a surviving piece by the Li school of artists; it is a pity that there is no definite inscription by which this can be verified.  His artistic school, along with that of Cao Zhibai, Zhu Derun, Tang Di, and others, all derive from Guo Xi by way of Zhao Mengfu. Inscribed by Wu Hufan in the winter month of the Bingzi year (1936).",
      "inscription_remark": "Inscription on the silk mount by Wu Hufan",
      "sortorder": 3
    },
    {
      "inscription": null,
      "inscription_translation": "Li Sheng was a native of Fengyang [Anhui province]. He excelled at ink bamboo painting and was as famous as his contemporaries Li Kan and Ke Jiusi. In landscape painting, he followed the style of Guo Xi, proving particularly skilled at the \"level distance\" perspective. He also painted landscapes featuring weathered rocks. An entry on him can be found in the Tuhui Baojian (Precious Mirror for Examining Painting).\n\nWu Yuqing was a native of Yihuang [Jiangxi province] and was skilled in both poetry and prose. In the sixth year of the Yongle reign (1408), he was recommended for the post of Drafting Official in the Central Drafting Office. He later served as Right Administration Vice Commissioner and concurrently as an Edict drafter. Critics described his small standard script as being like \"beautiful hairpins made of flowers,\" and his large cursive script as resembling \"auspicious clouds flying in the sky or rushing water flowing into a ravine.\"- From the Peiwenzhai Shuhuapu (Peiwen Studio's Compendium of Calligraphy and Painting).",
      "inscription_remark": "Inscription by Wu Hufan",
      "sortorder": 4
    },
    {
      "inscription": null,
      "inscription_translation": "An authentic handscroll on paper, Vimalakīrti Expounding the Dharma by Li Sheng. Its trees and rocks are exceedingly in the manner of Cao Zhibai and Xu Ben; the figures, meanwhile, can be discussed in the same breath as those by Ren Renfa and Yan Hui. It is a work of the highest caliber among Yuan dynasty masters...\nThe Heart Sūtra in standard script by Wu Yuqing is at once fluidly elegant and meticulously structured, its lineage deriving from Chu Suiliang of Henan's Scripture of Western Ascension (Xisheng Jing). The calligrapher was a Yuan dynasty man who later served as an official in the Ming...\nThe scroll was formerly in the collection of the family of Minister Xu Qianxue of Kunshan and is a surviving artifact from his Chuanshi Lou. Acquired in Shanghai in the sixth month of the Jisi year (1929). Inscribed by Wu Hufan at the Plum Shadow Studio [sic].",
      "inscription_remark": "Inscription by Wu Hufan",
      "sortorder": 5
    },
    {
      "inscription": null,
      "inscription_translation": "I recently viewed nine Yuan dynasty scrolls from the former Qing imperial collection. The sections by Zhao Shanchang and Lin Zhihuan displayed brushwork that was also profound and substantial, ranking them alongside Li Sheng. They all follow on master Gao Kegong. Could this perhaps be the result of a prevailing artistic trend of the time? Further inscribed by Chouyi [Wu Hufan].\nEighth day of the tenth month, Jisi year (1929), while testing a new long-bristled wolf-hair brush.",
      "inscription_remark": "Inscription by Wu Hufan",
      "sortorder": 6
    }
  ],
